/usr/bin/entab from the Micronix 1.6 distribution

There is no source for entab, so this is the disassembly, and entab.c is that
disassembly read back into C.

    disas -f entab.ctl entab.dist

entab.dist is the binary off inode 56 of disks/dist/1012-8_dist_2.IMD, 4256
bytes, mode 0711, dated 7 August 1982.

Unlike its three neighbours - detab, expand, compress, none of which has a
header - this one is a Whitesmith a.out: 99 94, a sixteen byte header, text
at 0100 for 0f31 bytes and data at 2000 for 015f.  It is also built with the
older of the two runtimes, the one login and init have, and not the one
newuser has: c.ent at 0fd6, c.ent2 at 0fe1 saving the register variable cells
at 2159, 215b and 215d.  So it is a different compile from the rest, and the
library it carries is login's.

What it is: one function, main at 010e, which is a case for every character,
and one helper at 031b that is the whole of the skill.  Two columns are
kept, the input's (the word at 2002) and the output's (2000); characters
that are blank or tab or backspace move the first and write nothing, and a
visible character calls the helper to bring the second up to the first
before it is written.  The helper

    backs up with backspaces if the output is past the input
    writes a blank, not a tab, if the gap is exactly one column
    writes tabs for as long as a tab stop is not past the input column
    writes blanks for what is left

so that a gap of one is never a tab, even when the tab would have landed on
the spot.  Everything that is not a blank, a tab, a backspace, a newline or a
carriage return and is not in 041 to 0176 - a form feed, a byte over 0176 -
is written as it stands, after the same catch-up, and does not move either
column.  A newline or a carriage return writes itself and sets both columns
to 0.  The blanks at the end of a line and at the end of the file are never
written, because nothing visible ever asked for them.

The standard output is flushed at each newline when it is a terminal - the
flag test at 203e after the newline case - and entab.c leaves that to stdio.

The exit status is the same oddity as detab's, with a different number: the
last getc's value is left in BC, and for the end of the file that is 255.  The
original exited 255 every time; this exits 0.

Checked in usersim against the original on six thousand random characters of
blanks, tabs, newlines, backspaces, form feeds and bytes over 0176; on a
short text with runs of one to nine blanks and blank lines; and on an empty
file: the same bytes each time.
